To make this solution more efficient for handling very large amounts of text, you may want to consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This allows you to work with the text as if it were loaded into memory, but without actually consuming all available RAM. Here's how you can modify the code above to use memory-mapped files:

```matlab
% Open the files using memory mapping
hMap1 = mmopen('Text1.txt', 'r');
hMap2 = mmopen('Text2.txt', 'r');

% Read the contents of the files into mappable arrays
text1 = mmread(hMap1);
text2 = mmread(hMap2);

% Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));

% Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);

% Close the memory-mapped files
mmclose(hMap1);
mmclose(hMap2);

% Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```

In this modified version, we open the files using `mmopen`, which returns a handle to the memory-mapped file. We then read the contents of the files using `mmread`. Since these functions operate on memory-mapped files, they do not consume additional system resources beyond what is required to map the files into memory. Once we have finished working with the data, we close the handles using `mmclose`. This frees up the mapped regions of memory and releases any associated resources. By using memory-mapped files, we can process very large text files without running out of memory or experiencing performance issues due to disk I/O.

