Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
To implement the bubble sort algorithm in C++ with minimal memory usage and error prevention, we'll use a simple implementation that only requires an array and two indices to iterate over it. We'll also add some error handling for invalid inputs.

Here's the code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include <vector>

namespace json = nlohmann;

// Bubble sort function
void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    const int size = arr.size();
    bool swapped; // Flag to check if any elements were swapped during pass

    for (int I = 0; I < size - 1; i++) { // Outer loop to iterate over array length - 1
        swapped = false; // Initialize flag

        for (int j = 0; j < size - I - 1; j++) { // Inner loop to iterate over unsorted portion of array
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) { // If current element is greater than next element
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]); // Swap them
                swapped = true; // Set flag to true
            }
        }

        // If no swaps were made during pass, array is already sorted
        if (!swapped) {
            return;
        }
    }
}

// Function to parse JSON array and sort using bubble sort
void sortArray(const json& jsonObj) {
    std::vector<int> arr = jsonObj["array"]; // Extract array from JSON object
    bubbleSort(arr); // Sort the array using bubble sort
}

// Example usage
int main() {
    json obj = R"({
        "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
    })";
    sortArray(obj); // Call sorting function passing JSON object
    std::cout << obj["array"] << std::endl; // Print sorted JSON array
    return 0;
